</article>$$S$ is stable under filtered colimits. Then it is easy to see that the collection of weak equivalences in $$ is also stable under filtered colimits. If $S$ is also a combinatorial model category, then a bit more effort shows that the class of weak equivalences in $$ is perfect, in the sense of Definition \ref{perfequiv}. $<article class="env-remark" id="">
</article> 
</p>
<p>
We now introduce a bit of notation for working with $$S$-enriched categories. If $A$ is an object of $S$, we will let $[1]_A$ denote the $S$-enriched category having two objects $X$ and $Y$, with \[  \bHom _{[1]_{A}}(Z,Z') = \begin{cases} \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{S}} &amp;  \text{if } Z=Z'=X \\ \mathbf{1}_{\mathbf{S}} &amp;  \text{if } Z=Z'=Y \\ A &amp;  \text{if } Z=X, Z'=Y \\ \emptyset &amp;  \text{if } Z=Y, Z'=X.\end{cases} \] Here $∅$ denotes the initial object of $S$ and $<strong>1</strong>_S$ denotes the unit object with respect to the monoidal structure on $S$. We will denote $[1]_ <strong>1</strong>_S $ simply by $[1]_S$. We let $[0]_S$ denote the $S$-enriched category having only a single object $X$, with $_*(X,X)=<strong>1</strong>_S$.\index{not}{1subS@}$[1]_A$$1bfs@$$[1]_S$$ 0bfs@$$[0]_S$$ 
</p>
<p>
We let $$C_0$ denote the collection of all morphisms in $S$ of the following types: \begin{itemize} \end{itemize}$
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(i)$"><p>
The inclusion $$∅↪[0]_S$. \item [$(ii)$] The induced maps $[1]_S →[1]_S'$, where $S →S'$ ranges over a set of generators for the weakly saturated class of cofibrations in $S$. $
</p>
<ul>
  
</ul>
<p>
<article class="env-proposition" id="5606B3">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/5606B3">Proposition <span data-tag="5606B3">8.3.2.4</span>.</a>model category!of simplicial categories Let </p>
</article>$$S$ be a combinatorial monoidal model category. Assume that every object of $S$ is cofibrant, and that the collection of weak equivalences in $S$ is stable under filtered colimits. Then there exists a left proper, combinatorial model structure on $$, characterized by the following conditions: \begin{itemize} \end{itemize}$
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(C)$"><p>
The class of cofibrations in $$$ is the smallest weakly saturated class of morphisms containing the set of morphisms $C_0$ appearing above. \item [$(W)$] The weak equivalences in $$ are defined as in \S \ref{equivdefequiv}. $
</p>
<ul>
  
</ul>
<p>
 <article class="env-proposition" id="">
</article> 
</p>
<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      It suffices to verify the hypotheses of Proposition <a href="/tag/8C7DD0" data-tag="8C7DD0">8.2.6.13</a>. Condition 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>
<p>
$$(1)$ follows from Remark \ref{swunk}. For condition $(3)$, we must show that any functor $F: →'$ having the right lifting property with respect to all morphisms in $C_0$ is a weak equivalence. Since $F$ has the right lifting property with respect to $i: ∅→[0]_S$, it is surjective on objects and therefore essentially surjective. The assumption that $F$ has the right lifting property with respect to the remaining morphisms of $C_0$ guarantees that for every $X, Y $, the induced map \[ \bHom _{\calC }(X,Y) \rightarrow \bHom _{\calC '}(F(X),F(Y)) \] is a trivial fibration in $S$, and therefore a weak equivalence. 

It remains to verify condition 

$(2)$: namely, that the class of weak equivalences is stable under pushout by the elements of $C_0$. We must show that given any pair of functors $F: →$, $G: →'$ with $F$ a weak equivalence and $G$ a pushout of some morphism in $C_0$, the induced map $F': ' →' = ∐_ '$ is a weak equivalence. There are two cases to consider. 

First, suppose that 

$G$ is a pushout of the generating cofibration $i: ∅→*$. In other words, the category $'$ is obtained from $$ by adjoining a new object $X$, which admits no morphisms to or from the objects of $$ (and no endomorphisms other than the identity). The category $'$ is obtained from $$ by adjoining $X$ in the same fashion. It is easy to see that if $F$ is a weak equivalence, then $F'$ is also a weak equivalence. 

The other basic case to consider is one in which 

$G$ is a pushout of one of the generating cofibrations $[1]_S →[1]_T$, where $S →T$ is a cofibration in $S$. Let $H: [1]_S →$ denote the ``attaching map'', so that $H$ is determined by a pair of objects $x= H(X)$ and $y=H(Y)$ and a map of $h: S →_(x,y)$. By definition, $'$ is universal with respect to the property that it receives a map from $$, and the map $h$ extends to a map $h: T →_'(x,y)$. To carry out the proof, we will give an explicit construction of a $S$-enriched category $'$ which has this universal property. 

For the remainder of the proof, we will assume that 

$S$ is the category of simplicial sets. This is purely for notational convenience; the same arguments can be employed without change in the general case. 

We begin by declaring that the objects of 

$'$ are the objects of $$. The definition of the morphisms in $'$ is a bit more complicated. Let $w$ and $z$ be objects of $$. We define a sequence of simplicial sets $M_'^k$ as follows: \[ M_{\calC }^0 = \bHom _{\calC }(w,z) \] \[ M_{\calC }^1 = \bHom _{\calC }(y,z) \times T \times \bHom _{\calC }(w,x) \] \[ M_{\calC }^2 = \bHom _{\calC }(y,z) \times T \times \bHom _{\calC }(y,x) \times T \times \bHom _{\calC }(w,x) \] and so forth. More specifically, for $k ≥1$, the $m$-simplices of $M_^k$ are finite sequences \[ ( \sigma _0, \tau _1, \sigma _1, \tau _2, \ldots , \tau _ k, \sigma _ k) \] where $σ_0 _(y,z)_m$, $σ_k _(w,x)_m$, $σ_i _(y,x)_m$ for $0 &amp;lt; i &amp;lt; k$, and $τ_i T_m$ for $1 ≤i ≤k$. 

We define 

$_'(w,z)$ to be the quotient of the disjoint union $ ∐_k M_^k$ by the equivalence relation which is generated by making the identification \[ ( \sigma _0, \tau _1, \ldots , \sigma _ k) \simeq (\sigma _0, \tau _1, \ldots , \tau _{j-1}, \sigma _{j-1} \circ h(\tau _ j) \circ \sigma _{j}, \tau _{j+1}, \ldots , \sigma _ k ) \] whenever the simplex $τ_j$ belongs to $S_m ⊆T_m$. 

We equip 

$'$ with an associative composition law, which is given on the level of simplices by \[ ( \sigma _0, \tau _1, \ldots , \sigma _ k ) \circ (\sigma '_0, \tau '_1, \ldots , \sigma '_ l) = (\sigma _0, \tau _1, \ldots , \tau _ k, \sigma _ k \circ \sigma '_0, \tau '_1, \ldots , \sigma '_ l). \] It is easy to verify that this composition law is well-defined (that is, compatible with the equivalence relation introduced above), associative, and that the identification $M_^0 = _(w,z)$ gives rise to an inclusion of categories $⊆'$. Moreover, the map $h: S →_(x,y)$ extends to $h: T →_'(x,y)$, given by the composition \[  T \simeq \{  \id _ y \}  \times T \times \{  \id _ x\}  \subseteq \bHom _{\calC }(y,y) \times T \times \bHom _{\calC }(x,x) = M_{\calC }^1 \rightarrow \bHom _{\calC '}(x,y). \] Moreover, it is not difficult to see that $'$ has the desired universal property. 

We observe that, by construction, the simplicial sets 

$_'(w,z)$ come equipped with a natural filtration. Namely, define $_'(w,z)^k$ to be the image of \[  \coprod _{0 \leq i \leq k} M_{\calC }^ i  \] in $_'(w,z)$. Then we have \[  \bHom _{\calC }(w,z) = \bHom _{\calC '}(w,z)^0 \subseteq \bHom _{\calC '}(w,z)^1 \subseteq \ldots  \] and $_k _'(w,z)^k = _'(w,z)$. Moreover, the inclusion $ _'(w,z)^k ⊆_'(w,z)^k+1$ is a pushout of the inclusion $N_^k+1 ⊆M_^k+1$, where $N^k+1$ is the simplicial subset of $M_^k+1$ whose $m$-simplices consist of those $(2m+1)$-tuples $(σ_0, τ_1, …, σ_m)$ such that $τ_i S_m$ for at least one value of $i$. 

Let us now return to the problem at hand: namely, we wish to prove that 

$F': ' →'$ is an equivalence. We note that the construction outlined above may also be employed to produce a model for $'$, and an analogous filtration on its morphism spaces. 

Since 

$G': →'$ and $F: →$ are essentially surjective, we deduce that $F'$ is essentially surjective. Hence it will suffice to show that, for any objects $w,z '$, the induced map \[ \phi : \bHom _{\calC '}(w,z) \rightarrow \bHom _{\calD '}(w,z) \] is a weak homotopy equivalence. For this, it will suffice to show that for each $i ≥0$, the induced map $φ_i: _'(w,z)^i →_'(w,z)^i$ is a weak homotopy equivalence; then $φ$, being a filtered colimit of weak homotopy equivalences $φ_i$, will itself be a weak homotopy equivalence. 

The proof now proceeds by induction on 

$i$. When $i=0$, $φ_i$ is a weak homotopy equivalence by assumption (since $F$ is an equivalence of simplicial categories). For the inductive step, we note that $φ_i+1$ is obtained as a pushout \[  \bHom _{\calC '}(w,z)^ i \coprod _{ N^{i+1}_{\calC }} M^{i+1}_{\calC } \rightarrow \bHom _{\calD '}(w,z)^ i \coprod _{ N^{i+1}_{\calD } } M^{i+1}_{\calD }. \] Since $S$ is left-proper, both of these pushouts are homotopy pushouts. Consequently, to show that $φ_i+1$ is a weak equivalence, it suffices to show that $φ_i$ is a weak equivalence and the each of the maps \[  N^{i+1}_{\calC } \rightarrow N^{i+1}_{\calD } \] \[  M^{i+1}_{\calC } \rightarrow M^{i+1}_{\calD } \] are weak equivalences. These statements follow easily from the compatibility of the monoidal structure of $S$ with the model structure, and the assumption that every object of $S$ is cofibrant. $
</p>
