In order for Proposition <a href="/tag/5606B3" data-tag="5606B3">8.3.2.4</a> to be useful in practice, we need to understand the fibrations in $$$. For this, we first introduce a few definitions. 

\begin{thmenv} \label{qfibb} Let 

\end{thmenv}

$F: →$ be a functor between ordinary categories. We will say that $F$ is a {\it quasi-fibration} if, for every object $C $ and every isomorphism $f: F(X) →Y$ in $$, there exists an isomorphism $f: X →Y$ in $$ such that $F( f ) = f$. $<article class="env-definition" id="">
</article> 
